# GET_VWIRE carrying target-to-controller groups in both data formats.
#
# HAND BUILT FROM THE SPECIFICATION, NOT FROM THE CAPTURE. The capture only
# ever answers GET_VWIRE with a single group at index 05h or 40h, so nothing
# independent confirms these bytes.
#
# Four groups, count byte 03h. Indices 4 and 6 are the System Event indices
# Tables 11 and 13 mark Target to Controller; indices 0 and 1 are Interrupt
# events, which p.60 says are "defined from target to controller only".
#
#   04h data D4h -- PME# asserted (active low), WAKE# released, OOB_RST_ACK
#                   released (active high). Bit 1 is RSV and its valid bit 5
#                   is clear.
#   06h data F6h -- every valid bit set, so all four wires carry a state. SCI#
#                   asserted, the rest released.
#   00h data 82h -- NOT a valid/level byte. Bit 7 is the interrupt level and
#                   bits 6:0 are the line, so this is IRQ 2 asserting. Decoded
#                   as valid/level it would come out as two plausible wires.
#   01h data 03h -- the same layout in the second bank: index 1 covers IRQ 128
#                   to 255, so line 03h is IRQ 131, and it is deasserting.
#
# CRC bytes pasted from tools/crc_ref.py (rule R4): 1Bh over {05}, 0Fh over the
# response. That response CRC is 0Fh by arithmetic, which is also the
# WAIT_STATE encoding -- it is read as a CRC because of where it sits, and a
# decoder that scanned for 0Fh anywhere in the phase would break here.
CMD 05 1B
TAR
RSP 08 03 04 D4 06 F6 00 82 01 03 0F 01 0F
